# Verification loop

When this project has a `.agent/loop.json`, it declares exactly how to check your work. Use it instead of guessing commands.

```bash
loop fast     # seconds — after an edit
loop test     # after a unit of work
loop full     # before you claim the work is done
```

Resolve `loop` to the first of these that exists:

1. `./node_modules/.bin/loop`
2. `node ~/.cursor/loop/loop.mjs`
3. `npx -y github:gastigonzalez1999/agent-dotfiles loop`

Exit codes: **0** green · **1** a check failed · **2** configuration or environment problem.

## When

- After any non-trivial edit — `loop fast`
- Before reporting work complete or opening a PR — `loop full` must exit 0
- No `.agent/loop.json`? Read the `loop-init` skill and create one before relying on ad-hoc commands.

## Do

- Read the **first** error, not the last — compilers cascade.
- Fix the cause. Re-run the **same** tier.
- Read `.agent/.loop-last.json` for full output rather than re-running commands by hand.
- Treat exit code 2 as an environment problem — fix the setup, not the source.
- Stop and escalate after `budget.maxIterations` (default 5) failed cycles on one gate.

## Don't

- Don't claim work is done on the strength of a `fast` run. `full` is the bar.
- Don't silence a check to make it pass — no `any`, no `@ts-expect-error`, no loosened assertion.
- Don't edit a test so it matches broken behaviour.
- Don't invent commands when the contract already names them.

## Note

Cursor has no hook system, so nothing enforces this — unlike Claude Code, where a Stop hook blocks finishing while the gate is red. Here it is on you to run the loop.
